The numeric parser uses pow and division, which need not produce exactly
identical intermediates on x87. Keep every valid/invalid input check, but
allow one double epsilon of relative error for finite expected values.
NaN and infinity must still fail when a finite result is expected, and
expected zero still requires exactly zero.

--- a/src/parser/numeric_parser_test.cpp
+++ b/src/parser/numeric_parser_test.cpp
@@ -45,7 +45,9 @@ bool run_checks(const std::vector<check>& checks)
         }
         else
         {
-            if (v != c.expected)
+            // pow/division can retain excess precision on x87.
+            const double tolerance = std::numeric_limits<double>::epsilon() * std::abs(c.expected);
+            if (!(std::isfinite(v) && std::abs(v - c.expected) <= tolerance))
             {
                 cout << "'" << c.str << "' was expected to be parsed as (" << c.expected << "), but the parser parsed it as (" << v << ")" << endl;
                 return false;
